description.narrative:
The annual narrative report for 2023 was submitted by Deme-So on 22 March 2024 (due by 31 March according to agreement). It is organized along four main sub-headings:

1. an analysis of the general situation in the country and implementation sites,
2. activities carried out and major achievements,
3. a table comparing objectives and actual activities,
4. challenges for the programme in 2023outlook for the programme in 2024. 
Under sub-heading one, Deme-So describes certain key changes in the context and general challenges in implementing the program, including:
- the sudden withdrawal of MINUSMA led to an increase in attacks, in particular in the central and northern parts of the country;
- arrests continued of dissidents, suspected of not supporting the transition or displaying any kind of opposition to the action of the transitional authorities;
- civic space kept shrinking with i.a. dissolution of associations advocating for the return to original constitutional order;
- certain cooperation partners decided to cease or decrease their support.
 However, the programme is implemented in particular in the south of the country, hence not the areas most affected by increased levels of violence during 2023. 
Some specific results reported include:
- 136 paralegals trained who in turn led 3 267 debating and sensitization sessions on rights and access to justice in different locations in the regions of Kayes, Nioro and Sikasso, which reached a total of 84 941 people of which 69% were women. As a consequence, people were better equipped to claim their rights through the justice system, and to use public services, such as requesting a birth certificate within the legal time frame without having to pay the fee associated with late registration;
- the 136 paralegals gave 3 269 counselling sessions, of which 1 601 women (49%), in the 68 municipalities where the program was implemented;
- 952 mediation sessions were organized, notably to support mediation between pastoralists and farmers;
- 10 prisons in Sikasso, Koutiala, Yorosso, Kati, San, Tominian, Macina, Kadiolo, Kolondièba and Bougouni received vocational training facilities which made it possible to ensure a professional future for 112 prisoners and to humanize life in prisons;
- 237 people, including 148 women, received a counselling session from a lawyer in 2023;
The above results follow the initial work plan and the budget distribution between different types of activities. Regarding evaluation and impact measurement of the programme, it can be noted that the planned baseline study (result R3.2) has been carried out as planned during 2023.

6 prisons have been equipped with workshops for sewing, dyeing, leatherwood, metal and wood carpentry, fish farming and wire mesh manufacture.

Provision of legal and judicial assistance 

78 people, including 46 women, benefited from the services of a lawyer during the reporting period.
